::JOUR_U04_L04_Q_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.MED.04] Source: Topic: Should the school create a phone-free lunch area? Argument A: The school should create a phone-free lunch area because many students need a place to talk without screens taking over the table. A guidance counselor says some students report feeling ignored when everyone around them is scrolling. A pilot table in the media center attracted twenty students during one week, and several said it helped them meet classmates. Supporters say the area would be optional, so students who want to use phones at lunch could still sit elsewhere. Argument B: The school should not create a phone-free lunch area because lunch is one of the few flexible times students have during the day. Many students use phones to check family messages, manage club reminders, or finish quick tasks. A student council survey found that sixty-eight percent of respondents opposed lunch phone restrictions. Opponents argue that a separate area may divide students instead of teaching respectful conversation habits for all tables. Question 01: Which choice accurately compares the two claims? {
~A. Both arguments claim that phones should be banned from the entire school.#Not correct. Choice A says 'Both arguments claim that phones should be banned from the entire school.', which does not fully answer this specific journalism task. Recheck the source details, the question wording, and the mapped standard before choosing the best supported answer.
=B. Argument A supports creating a phone-free lunch area, while Argument B opposes creating one.#Correct. Step 1: identify the standard skill being tested. Step 2: use the embedded source and lesson model to justify the answer. This option is strongest because it connects purpose, rhetoric, language, claim, evidence, or validity to the source rather than guessing from topic alone.
~C. Argument A and Argument B make the same claim in different words.#Not correct. Choice C says 'Argument A and Argument B make the same claim in different words.', which does not fully answer this specific journalism task. Recheck the source details, the question wording, and the mapped standard before choosing the best supported answer.
~D. Argument B supports the phone-free area, while Argument A opposes it.#Not correct. Choice D says 'Argument B supports the phone-free area, while Argument A opposes it.', which does not fully answer this specific journalism task. Recheck the source details, the question wording, and the mapped standard before choosing the best supported answer.
}
::JOUR_U04_L04_Q_Q02::[MLA.JOUR.NEWS.02] Source: Topic: Should the school create a phone-free lunch area? Argument A: The school should create a phone-free lunch area because many students need a place to talk without screens taking over the table. A guidance counselor says some students report feeling ignored when everyone around them is scrolling. A pilot table in the media center attracted twenty students during one week, and several said it helped them meet classmates. Supporters say the area would be optional, so students who want to use phones at lunch could still sit elsewhere. Argument B: The school should not create a phone-free lunch area because lunch is one of the few flexible times students have during the day. Many students use phones to check family messages, manage club reminders, or finish quick tasks. A student council survey found that sixty-eight percent of respondents opposed lunch phone restrictions. Opponents argue that a separate area may divide students instead of teaching respectful conversation habits for all tables. Question 02: Which evidence most strengthens Argument A? {
